Subject: [PATCH 19/44] docs: p0 sits on the model's own noise floor

The prose prompt's 19/64 is exactly transformers' own fp32-versus-bf16
agreement on the same weights (39, 48, 19 across the three prompts), so that
prompt's diverging token is where the reference disagrees with itself. Records
the number and what raising it would require.
---
 docs/recipes/sensenova-loop-9b-metal.md | 16 ++++++++++------
 zig/tests/qwen27/mhc_check.zig          |  2 +-
 2 files changed, 11 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/recipes/sensenova-loop-9b-metal.md b/docs/recipes/sensenova-loop-9b-metal.md
index dce07a03b..46927f8e0 100644
--- a/docs/recipes/sensenova-loop-9b-metal.md
+++ b/docs/recipes/sensenova-loop-9b-metal.md
@@ -85,13 +85,17 @@ Compared against transformers at the pinned revision, run on the **same 4-bit we
 | arithmetic reasoning | **64/64** |
 | plain prose | 19/64 |
 
-Top-1 agrees on all three. The prose prompt breaks first at token 19 on a rank-2 candidate 1.19 logits behind, and
-the per-execution error curve is continuous rather than stepped, with the same character on the prompt that passes,
-so this is a numerical accumulation difference rather than a wiring fault. A 512-token prompt agrees for 14 of its
-first 16 tokens.
+Top-1 agrees on all three. The prose prompt breaks first at token 19 on a rank-2 candidate 1.19 logits behind; its
+per-execution error curve is continuous rather than stepped, with the same character on the prompts that pass, so
+this is numerical accumulation rather than a wiring fault. A 512-token prompt agrees for 14 of its first 16 tokens.
 
-For reference, transformers' own BF16-versus-FP32 agreement on the same weights is 39/64, 48/64 and 64/64 — the
-model's own noise floor is not far from where the engine sits on the hardest prompt.
+The prose prompt sits exactly on the model's own noise floor. Transformers run on the same 4-bit weights in fp32
+against itself in bf16 agrees for **39/64**, **48/64** and **19/64** on the same three prompts — the same 19 for
+prose that the engine reaches against the bf16 reference. That prompt is one where the bf16 run diverges from its
+own fp32 twin at the same token, so 19/64 there is the measurement's resolution, not a defect to chase.
+
+Known gap: raising the engine above that floor would need the reference to be the fp32 run, not the bf16 one this
+recipe measures against.
 
 ## What is not covered
 
